From 0000000000000000000000000000000000000000 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: ysfaculty <haejin.cho@yonsei.ac.kr>
Date: Wed, 11 Nov 2026 23:03:55 +0900
Subject: [PATCH 188/194] hjviewer: only SYSTEM and the session's user can use
 the connection window's pipe, and the service checks who serves it

The connection window (--cm) serves \\.\pipe\HJViewer\query_cm, the
pipe over which the service's process (--server, SYSTEM when installed)
hands it each incoming connection: the controller's name and ID, chat,
file transfer jobs and blocks, clipboard files, privacy-mode state, and
over which the window answers "click to accept" and ends a connection.
1.9.3 protected the main and the service pipe but left this one as it
was: the window made it with the default security descriptor
(src/ui_cm_interface.rs start_ipc -> ipc::new_listener("_cm"), which
used SecurityAttributes::empty()), and the --server connected with
Endpoint::connect and took whatever answered (src/server/connection.rs
start_ipc, src/server/clipboard_service.rs, src/privacy_mode.rs). On a
PC used by several Windows accounts another, non-admin account could
make that name while no window was open and be handed the next
connection's data, answer for the PC's user, or accept a connection.

The pipe now goes through the 1.9.3 helpers of src/ipc/windows.rs, as
the main pipe does (src/ipc.rs is_windows_protected_ipc, used by
new_listener and connect):
- listener: owner = the window's user, protected DACL with everything
  for SYSTEM and that owner and, unlike the main pipe, nothing for
  Everyone: only the --server (SYSTEM, or the same user for an app that
  is not installed) and the main window (theme, language) connect to
  it. The first-instance flag of parity-tokio-ipc stays. A window whose
  descriptor cannot be built does not start, as for the main pipe.
- client: opens with the client access only and, before a byte of a
  message goes out, requires a restricted DACL and an owner that is
  SYSTEM or the expected user, then the server's session = the
  client's session and the server's executable = the client's own. The
  expected user is the client's own user, except for a SYSTEM client:
  there it is the user of the token the service starts the window with
  (platform::windows::session_user_sid_string -> get_user_token(session,
  true), the token of run_as_user -> run_exe_in_session), and the
  "not less elevated" rule is skipped, since that window runs
  unelevated by design. Main and service pipes are checked as before;
  the expected owner is only computed when the owner is not SYSTEM.

Mixed versions (the window's pipe only; the 1.9.3 notes cover the
others):
- A 1.9.4 client takes no 1.9.3 window's pipe (the default DACL gives
  Administrators and the logon session more than client access). A
  1.9.3 client opens a 1.9.4 window's pipe: SYSTEM through the SY entry,
  the same user through the owner entry.
- Unattended update 1.9.3 -> 1.9.4: the new package's update_me stops
  the service and ends every HJViewer.exe in every session
  (src/platform/windows.rs update_me, taskkill /F /IM) before it copies
  and starts the new service, so no 1.9.3 window is left; the next
  incoming connection starts a 1.9.4 window. A connection that was open
  drops and comes back as with 1.9.3. The installer does the same
  (get_before_uninstall). A 1.9.3 service with a 1.9.4 window cannot
  arise from that order, and would work.
- An old app that is not installed next to the installed one does not
  host (the installed service holds the main pipe), so it starts no
  window. An app that is not installed hosting alone, and an installed
  main window hosting while the service is stopped, start their window
  as the same user, in the same session, from the same file: accepted;
  the service's --server takes that window after "start service" too.
- What changes: a window of another Windows session, of another copy
  of the app or of an older version is refused where 1.9.3 used it (it
  showed this session's connections to another user). The next commit
  makes the --server wait for such a window to go.

Left alone: the window does not check its clients beyond the DACL; a
process of the same account can still add instances, and another
account can still take the name while no window is open, which denies
the window (the connection ends with "connection manager error") but
gets no data; the other postfixes keep their rules.

